$OctThreeArticles = @(
  @{
    Slug = 'perforated-roll-labels-hand-packing-guide'
    Title = 'Perforated Roll Labels for Hand-Packing Teams'
    Description = 'Plan perforated label rolls around tear position, label spacing, hand dispensing, roll packing and a real packing-bench trial.'
    Category = 'Manual label application'
    CardDescription = 'Let a packer separate one seal cleanly without disturbing the next label on the roll.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of original illustrated tea box seal labels on a perforated roll being torn at a packing bench'
    Caption = 'The perforation belongs between labels, but the hand-packing motion decides exactly where.'
    Published = '2026-10-03'
    Faq = @(
      @('Are perforated rolls compatible with automatic applicators?', 'Do not assume so. A tear line can affect web strength and sensing; confirm the applicator requirements and test a proposed roll on the actual machine.'),
      @('Can a perforation run through the printed sticker?', 'That would change the finished label. Specify whether the perforation is in the liner between labels or intentionally through a detachable label section.'),
      @('How should a buyer specify the roll?', 'Share label size, gap, core, winding, count per roll, tear position and the intended hand dispenser or packing motion.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>On a hand-packing bench, a worker may need one seal now and another in ten seconds. A continuous roll can be convenient, but a loose strip of backing soon curls around boxes and elbows. I would ask the packer to show how the label leaves the roll before deciding whether a tear line belongs in the construction.</p>
  <p>A perforated roll usually lets the operator separate a short length of liner between labels. It does not automatically make the sticker itself easier to peel, and it is not interchangeable with a slit backing or a dry lift tab. The decision starts with the sequence: dispense, tear, peel, apply, then reach for the next piece.</p>
  <h2>Put the Tear Line Where Hands Need It</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Question</th><th>Ask the packing team</th><th>Proof check</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>One label or a strip?</td><td>How many are pulled at once?</td><td>Perforation interval</td></tr><tr><td>Where is the roll?</td><td>On a spindle, dispenser or tabletop?</td><td>Tear angle and web tension</td></tr><tr><td>What is applied?</td><td>Box seal, product label or bag closure?</td><td>Adhesive contact area</td></tr><tr><td>What is thrown away?</td><td>Individual liners or one waste roll?</td><td>Bench tidiness and disposal</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>For a tea box seal with an original leaf illustration, the printed design should stay fully on the label. The perforation should not nick its edge or split the wordmark. Ask for a proof that marks label cutline, liner gap and perforation separately. A tidy visual mockup cannot show whether the web tears too easily during unwinding.</p>
  <h2>Test Strength Against Convenience</h2>
  <p>A deep perforation may tear beautifully when held in the hand but break while the roll is being mounted. A shallow one may require two hands and an awkward tug. Request a short production-style roll and let staff use the real dispenser, gloves and carton position. Watch for the next label lifting when the previous segment is torn away.</p>
  <p>State the roll core, outer diameter limit, winding direction and labels per roll. The right label artwork on the wrong winding can still slow the table. If the same labels will later run through an automatic applicator, treat that as a separate compatibility review rather than assuming the hand-packing construction will transfer.</p>
  <h2>Brief the Supplier With the Whole Motion</h2>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Send a photo of the packing station and dispenser</li><li>Confirm finished label size and liner gap</li><li>Mark the perforation on a dieline</li><li>Specify roll core, winding and maximum diameter</li><li>Trial tearing, peeling and application in sequence</li><li>Check that the next printed label remains undamaged</li></ul>
  <p>The <a href="/blog/label-roll-unwind-direction-core-size-guide/">roll setup guide</a> explains core and winding choices; the <a href="/blog/easy-peel-tab-stickers-guide/">easy-peel guide</a> covers a different handling problem. Share the dispenser and tear position through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">label specification planner</a>. A good perforation is almost invisible to the customer and obvious to the hand using it.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'tapered-bottle-label-sizing-guide'
    Title = 'Tapered Bottle Labels: Size Around the Curve'
    Description = 'Measure tapered bottles for custom labels using real container samples, top-to-bottom circumference, seam clearance and wrap trials.'
    Category = 'Bottle label fit'
    CardDescription = 'A rectangular label can wrinkle on a bottle that narrows, even when both dimensions seem correct.'
    Alt = 'Photographic label-fit test with an original illustrated citrus drink label on a tapered glass bottle and a paper wrap template'
    Caption = 'A straight label meets a curved surface differently at its top and bottom edges.'
    Published = '2026-10-03'
    Faq = @(
      @('Why does a rectangular label wrinkle on a tapered bottle?', 'The upper and lower circumferences differ, so the flat label edges travel different distances around the container. Measure both and trial a shaped template.'),
      @('Can one label fit several bottle suppliers?', 'Only after checking the actual dimensions and panel geometry of each bottle. Similar stated capacity does not guarantee the same taper or usable label area.'),
      @('Should I send a bottle drawing or a physical sample?', 'Send both if possible. A physical sample reveals ridges, seams, taper and the real application path that a drawing may not communicate.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A buyer measures the middle of a bottle, chooses a label width and is surprised by a diagonal crease after application. The bottle is not necessarily defective and the print may be perfectly accurate. The upper and lower edges simply have different distances to travel around a tapered wall. I would stop adjusting the adhesive until the geometry is understood.</p>
  <p>Start with a real empty bottle, not only the supplier's volume specification. Mark the intended top and bottom edges of the label, then measure the circumference at both heights. Note shoulders, molded seams, grip panels and any embossing. A capacity label such as 250 ml tells you almost nothing about usable print area.</p>
  <h2>Measure at the Label's Actual Heights</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Measurement</th><th>Why it matters</th><th>How to verify</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Top circumference</td><td>Upper wrap path</td><td>Flexible tape at top edge</td></tr><tr><td>Bottom circumference</td><td>Lower wrap path</td><td>Flexible tape at bottom edge</td></tr><tr><td>Usable height</td><td>Clear of shoulder and heel</td><td>Paper mockup on bottle</td></tr><tr><td>Seam allowance</td><td>Prevents collision or a thick overlap</td><td>Photograph applied sample</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>Wrap a paper template around the bottle and draw a level reference line while it is on the container. Remove the paper and inspect its shape. It may not be a rectangle. For a heavily tapered bottle, the artwork and dieline may need a curved top and bottom. The goal is a label that appears level when applied, not necessarily one that looks rectangular in the flat file.</p>
  <h2>Proof Artwork After the Shape Is Settled</h2>
  <p>An original citrus branch illustration can follow a curved label edge, while the drink name remains in the flattest, most visible zone. Keep tiny type away from a deep taper or seam. If the bottle carries owner-approved ingredients or other required copy, make sure the final shaped panel still leaves enough readable space. The product owner must approve all claims and market wording.</p>
  <p>Test the actual face stock and adhesive on the intended bottle. A thin film may conform differently from a textured paper. That is a sample question, not a promise that one material always wins. Apply several labels with the method the packer will use and compare their position after normal handling and storage.</p>
  <h2>Control Bottle Variants Before Ordering Bulk</h2>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Send a physical bottle or accurate photos from every side</li><li>Measure circumference at top and bottom label edges</li><li>Mark seams, ridges, shoulder and heel</li><li>Make a full-size paper wrap before artwork approval</li><li>Apply a printed sample using the real process</li><li>Confirm each bottle supplier and size variant separately</li></ul>
  <p>See the <a href="/blog/how-to-choose-sticker-size-for-packaging/">label sizing guide</a> for basic usable-area planning and the <a href="/blog/custom-roll-labels-for-bottles-and-jars/">bottle roll-label guide</a> for production format. Share bottle dimensions and the paper wrap photo through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a>. The bottle decides the shape; the artwork can follow.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'retail-price-stickers-markdown-guide'
    Title = 'Retail Price Stickers: Change the Price, Keep the Pack'
    Description = 'Plan retail price and markdown stickers around removable adhesive, barcode clearance, shelf workflow, variant control and truthful pricing.'
    Category = 'Retail operations'
    CardDescription = 'Make a temporary price change clear to shoppers without damaging the product packaging.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of original illustrated stationery packs with neat printed sale price stickers placed clear of barcodes'
    Caption = 'A markdown label should be easy to see, easy to scan around and honest about the final price.'
    Published = '2026-10-03'
    Faq = @(
      @('Should a markdown sticker cover the original barcode?', 'Usually no. Keep scan paths and required information accessible, unless the retailer has approved a controlled replacement barcode workflow.'),
      @('Will a removable price sticker leave no residue?', 'No universal guarantee is possible. Test the exact adhesive on the printed box, film, paper or coated surface after a realistic dwell time.'),
      @('Can a sticker claim a percentage discount?', 'The retailer must verify the reference price, calculation and applicable market rules. The printer should reproduce only approved, current pricing copy.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A price change often happens after finished packs are already on a shelf. The store needs a shopper to spot the new price, a cashier to scan the correct item and a customer to remove the sticker without tearing a gift box. Those jobs compete for the same few square centimeters. I would photograph the real pack front and back before picking a sticker shape.</p>
  <p>A retail price sticker is not just a small poster. It is a temporary operational layer on top of another designed object. The retailer must own the actual price, discount math and timing. The label supplier should not invent a “was” price or imply a sale claim that the retailer has not approved.</p>
  <h2>Give the Sticker a Safe Placement Zone</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Pack feature</th><th>Avoid covering</th><th>Check at the shelf</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Front artwork</td><td>Product identity and variant</td><td>Shopper sees the correct item</td></tr><tr><td>Barcode</td><td>Quiet zone and scan path</td><td>Cashier scans first try</td></tr><tr><td>Required copy</td><td>Owner-approved warnings or details</td><td>Information remains readable</td></tr><tr><td>Opening seam</td><td>Unboxing or tamper feature</td><td>Sticker does not tear the pack</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>For illustrated stationery packs, a compact original price-tag icon and large numerals can be enough. Do not crowd a small circle with the old price, percentage, campaign date and legal copy unless the text remains legible at final size. The staff member applying it needs one unmistakable spot; inconsistent placement can make the shelf look untidy.</p>
  <h2>Test Removal on the Actual Printed Surface</h2>
  <p>A coated carton, a paper sleeve and a clear pouch can respond differently to the same adhesive. Apply samples to actual finished packaging, store them for a realistic interval and remove them as a shopper might. Check for ink lift, discoloration and residue. “Removable” is a category to trial, not a promise about every print finish.</p>
  <p>If the retailer uses price labels on many SKUs, set up a simple approval sheet: item code, current price, sticker version, quantity and placement photo. Confirm that old versions are removed from the packing area when pricing changes. The human problem is not only print quality; it is a roll with yesterday's number still sitting beside today's stock.</p>
  <h2>Approve the Shelf and Checkout Result</h2>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Supply retailer-approved pricing text and dates</li><li>Photograph the exact pack and barcode location</li><li>Check final-size legibility at shelf distance</li><li>Test adhesive hold and later removal</li><li>Scan the item after the sticker is applied</li><li>Control SKU versions and leftover label rolls</li></ul>
  <p>The <a href="/blog/removable-vs-permanent-stickers/">adhesive guide</a> explains removal trials; the <a href="/blog/custom-sticker-reorder-checklist/">reorder checklist</a> helps control changing files. Send pack photos, approved prices and application quantities through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a>. A price sticker succeeds when neither shopper nor cashier has to decode it.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'cosmetic-sample-sachet-labels-guide'
    Title = 'Cosmetic Sample Sachet Labels: Small Pack, Full Job'
    Description = 'Plan labels for cosmetic sample sachets around flexible film, seal edges, tiny text, product variants and owner-approved information.'
    Category = 'Beauty sample packaging'
    CardDescription = 'Protect the sachet seal and keep sample identity readable at a genuinely small size.'
    Alt = 'Photograph of colorful original botanical skincare sample labels on flexible sachets beside a matching printed label sheet'
    Caption = 'A sample label needs to survive a flexing pouch without intruding on its seals.'
    Published = '2026-10-03'
    Faq = @(
      @('Can one label fit every cosmetic sample sachet?', 'Not safely by assumption. Sachet dimensions, seal widths, film finish and fill shape vary; trial the label on the exact filled pack.'),
      @('Can tiny required text be placed on the front sticker?', 'The responsible brand must approve the required content and minimum readability for each market. If the space is insufficient, reconsider the pack or accompanying information rather than shrinking text blindly.'),
      @('Should the label cross a heat seal?', 'Do not place it there without package-owner review. Keep normal label placement clear of seal edges and tear notches so opening and seal integrity are not compromised.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A cosmetic sample sachet is easy to design at 300 percent zoom. In a customer's hand it is small, flexible and often slippery. The tear notch must work, the product variant must be obvious and the label cannot make the sealed edge behave differently. I would ask for a filled sachet before discussing a polished mockup.</p>
  <p>Measure the flat face, then mark heat seals, notch, crimping and the area that bulges after filling. A label placed across a seal edge may lift or interfere with opening. A label placed on the most flexible center may wrinkle. Those are packaging questions to test with the sachet owner, not details to guess from a PDF.</p>
  <h2>Choose a Real Usable Panel</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>Risk</th><th>Proof action</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Heat-sealed edge</td><td>Irregular contact or opening interference</td><td>Mark a clear setback</td></tr><tr><td>Tear notch</td><td>Sticker bridges opening path</td><td>Open an applied sample</td></tr><tr><td>Filled center</td><td>Flex and label wrinkling</td><td>Handle a filled sachet</td></tr><tr><td>Variable lot area</td><td>Information changes by batch</td><td>Agree on coding owner and method</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>An original botanical illustration can distinguish a calming blue variant from a bright citrus one, but the product name needs equal attention. Avoid using color as the only cue: a packer or shopper should still identify the sample under ordinary light. If a label sheet contains several variants, confirm the count and approved artwork version for each.</p>
  <h2>Keep Claims and Small Type Honest</h2>
  <p>The cosmetics brand is responsible for ingredient, warning, origin and other market-specific information. Do not invent a clinical claim or certification icon just to fill space. If the sachet cannot hold readable required information, the owner needs to decide whether an outer card, sleeve or different pack format is appropriate. The print proof should use supplied, approved copy.</p>
  <p>Check the smallest text at actual physical size on a printed proof. Contrast can change on metallic or translucent film. If the design uses a clear label, test it over the real sachet background; the color underneath may make fine letters disappear. A beautiful enlarged screen render is not a readability test.</p>
  <h2>Trial the Filled, Handled Sachet</h2>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Send filled sachet samples, not only a dieline</li><li>Mark seals, notch and flexing areas</li><li>Approve all product text and claims by variant</li><li>Proof tiny type at final print size</li><li>Test label adhesion after normal handling</li><li>Open the sachet with the label applied</li></ul>
  <p>The <a href="/blog/custom-cosmetic-labels-guide/">cosmetic packaging guide</a> covers larger formats; the <a href="/blog/white-ink-small-text-custom-stickers/">small-text and white-ink guide</a> helps with contrast. Share a real sachet photo and the approved copy through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a>. A small label still has a full job to do.</p>
'@
  },
  @{
    Slug = 'hot-foil-vs-metallic-ink-labels-guide'
    Title = 'Hot Foil vs Metallic Ink for Custom Labels'
    Description = 'Compare hot foil and metallic ink by artwork detail, tactile effect, cost drivers, curved packs and physical proof expectations.'
    Category = 'Premium label finishes'
    CardDescription = 'Choose the metallic effect for the way the pack will be handled and viewed, not just a screen mockup.'
    Alt = 'Photographic side-by-side original botanical gift labels showing a reflective hot-foil detail and a softer metallic-ink print'
    Caption = 'A metallic effect changes with light and viewing angle; a physical proof is the fair comparison.'
    Published = '2026-10-03'
    Faq = @(
      @('Is hot foil always shinier than metallic ink?', 'Often the effects differ, but exact appearance depends on foil, ink, substrate, coverage and light. Compare physical samples on the proposed label material.'),
      @('Can very small text be foiled?', 'Fine details need a production review. Request a final-size proof of the smallest strokes and spacing rather than assuming every digital detail will hold.'),
      @('Can a metallic effect be used on a curved bottle?', 'The finished label may be applied to a curve, but artwork position, creasing and substrate flexibility need a test on the real container.')
    )
    Body = @'
  <p>A gold swatch on a monitor promises almost everything and proves almost nothing. It does not show how a foil catches light on a shelf, whether a fine line breaks up, or whether a metallic ink looks subdued on rough paper. I would ask which part of the artwork needs attention before choosing a process.</p>
  <p>Hot foil generally applies a separate foil layer to selected areas, while metallic ink prints pigment as part of the ink system. Both can be useful. Neither is a universal premium upgrade. For a gift label, a small original botanical crest may deserve a bright accent; making every word reflective can make the pack harder to read.</p>
  <h2>Compare the Effect at the Intended Size</h2>
  <table><thead><tr><th>Decision</th><th>Hot foil question</th><th>Metallic ink question</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Visual effect</td><td>Is a distinct reflective highlight wanted?</td><td>Is a softer printed sheen enough?</td></tr><tr><td>Detail</td><td>Will thin strokes and tight gaps reproduce?</td><td>Will small type retain contrast?</td></tr><tr><td>Substrate</td><td>Does the chosen face accept the process?</td><td>How does the paper or film change color?</td></tr><tr><td>Run planning</td><td>Is a separate die or setup required?</td><td>How does ink coverage affect the result?</td></tr></tbody></table>
  <p>Ask the print supplier to review the actual vector artwork. Separate the metallic layer from ordinary colors and mark where overlap matters. Leave breathing room between foil details and tiny type. If the label wraps a jar or bottle, keep key metallic elements away from the seam and any strong bend until a sample has been applied.</p>
  <h2>Judge the Label on the Real Pack</h2>
  <p>Place a physical proof on the final carton, bottle or pouch. Look at it in daylight, store lighting and a close-up handling position. A glossy foil may compete with a glossy film; a matte paper may make a restrained metallic ink feel more deliberate. Check that required information remains readable. The product owner must approve all copy and claims, regardless of finish.</p>
  <p>Cost depends on artwork area, setup, material, quantity and production method. Do not use a generic claim that one effect is always cheaper. Request quotes for the same design, size and substrate, then compare the visual gain against the additional process. Sometimes reducing the metallic area improves both clarity and budget.</p>
  <h2>Send a Finish Brief That Can Be Quoted</h2>
  <ul class="check-list"><li>Provide editable vector art with metallic areas on a separate layer</li><li>State label dimensions, material and quantity</li><li>Identify the smallest lines and text</li><li>Send a photo of the real package and lighting context</li><li>Request physical samples of both finishes if possible</li><li>Approve the applied label, not only a flat swatch</li></ul>
  <p>Read the <a href="/blog/matte-vs-glossy-stickers/">matte versus gloss guide</a> for base finish and the <a href="/blog/custom-sticker-color-matching-guide/">color matching guide</a> for proof expectations. Send layered artwork and the package photo through the <a href="/label-specification-planner/">specification planner</a>. The best metallic detail supports the product name instead of demanding all the attention.</p>
'@
  }
)
